    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d102">

                <pb facs="0140" n="130" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg130"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5–1947: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-05-19T19:00:00+09:00">May 19, 1947—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 20—11:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1090. Following is Mukden’s 107 of May 16 to Nanking.</p>
                <p>“Information available here indicates NECC headquarters<anchor n="95"
                        xml:id="d102-f1"/> regards present Communist offensive as most serious yet
                    faced. Communist forces pushing westward from areas some 100 miles northeast
                    Mukden have taken Shangchengtze and are threatening Meihokow and Tsingyuan on
                    Mukden–Hailing railway. Observers believe these forces aim reach main
                    Harbin–Mukden line in region Seupingkai with view there meeting southward
                    pushing Communist forces now near Hwaite thus completing isolation Changchun.
                    Communist attacks to cut Mukden–Tientsin line near Shanghaikwan also believed
                    likely. <pb facs="0141" n="131" xml:id="pg131"/> (See Tientsin’s 103 of May 19
                    to Department<anchor n="96" xml:id="d102-f2"/> and Changchun’s 74 of May 19, 9
                    a.m. to Department.<anchor n="97" xml:id="d102-f3"/>) Nationalists reported
                    moving much needed troops from Tungliao area to reinforce other areas.
                        Ward”.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d102-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d102-f1" n="95" xml:id="d102-f1-content">
                <p>Headquarters of the Commander in Chief of the Chinese forces, Northeast China
                    Command.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d102-f2" n="96" xml:id="d102-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d102-f3" n="97" xml:id="d102-f3-content">
                <p>Repeated as No. 195 to Nanking, not printed; it concerned questions regarding
                    disposition of the Consulate code machine and materials in the event of Chinese
                    Communist capture of Changchun (119.25 Strip Cipher/5–1947).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d102-f4" n="98" xml:id="d102-f4-content">
                <p>Angus Ward, Consul General at Mukden.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
